  "query_preview": "..\n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nAnti-cyclonicly curved southwesterly flow aloft around large-scale \nridging centered east of the area will support sunny, warm and dry \nconditions today. Afternoon temperatures today and again tomorrow \nwill reach the lower to mid 80s for many areas with a few locations \nacross north-central MT reaching the upper 80s on Friday. \n\nAn upper level trough from the Pacific, moving onshore tomorrow, \nwill send a cold front across the Northern Rockies Friday evening \nwith gusty south winds developing late Friday ahead of the front and \nan abrupt end to the warmth as gusty west winds and much cooler air \nfollow the frontal passage Friday night through Saturday. Precipitation\nis fairly limited, though a brief period of showers with lowering\nsnow levels is likely to occur following the frontal passage over\nthe higher elevations in Glacier NP with some minor accumulation \n(up to 1\") possible over Logan Pass and elevations above 6000ft. A\nfew showers and isolated thunderstorms may also accompany the \nfront across north-central MT where little or no meaningful \nprecipitation expected. \n\nThe embedded shortwave disturbance responsible for the cold front \nlifts from NW MT into AB Friday night with gusty west winds \npersisting across much of the area through Saturday. Temperatures on \nSaturday will be around 20F cooler than those of Friday with \nafternoon temperatures generally in the mid to upper 60s. A \nprogressive upper level pattern looks to prevail into next week \nwith several embedded Pacific weather disturbances tracking into \nthe western US. Temperatures should remain near to slightly below \nseasonal averages, though there is still considerable uncertainty \nwith timing and extent of moisture with these features. \n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nWind continues to be the main concern in the late Friday through \nSaturday morning period. There remains potential for enhanced winds \nif/where any convection develops along the cold front Friday \nevening. While convection would locally increase the likelihood \nof gusts in excess of 50 mph, strong pressure rises behind the \nfront should support fairly widespread gusts in excess of 40 mph \nacross north-central MT in the wake of the front. \n\nA High Wind Watch has been issued for areas east of the Northern \nRocky Mtn Front through eastern Glacier and western Toole counties\nfor Friday night through Saturday morning. The probability for \ngusts exceeding 58 mph has increased to above 70% across eastern \nGlacier county including the Cut Bank area. This is supported by \nfavorable conditions for a period mountain wave enhancement Friday\nnight as a mid level wind maximum moves across the area just \nbehind the shortwave lifting into AB. Hoenisch",
